A||N||1||87ECO5||Nesting Falcons in Ohio||||14||
Z||000000||87ECO5||03-30-2001||02:18 PM||||For anyone interested in the Peregrine Falcon, Ohio (probably other states too) has a website you can visit that discusses the bird's comeback from extinction and even has a camera focused on the nest.<P>This link is for the Columbus pair - Bandit and Katie:<P> <A HREF="http://www.dnr.state.oh.us/odnr/wildlife/diversity/falcon/columbus/falcons.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dnr.state.oh.us/odnr/wildlife/diversity/falcon/columbus/falcons.html</A> <P>This site also has links to Cleveland and (I think) Cincinnati Falcon nesting sites.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Cheever2||03-30-2001||02:56 PM||||Just last night I was researching camping areas around Williamsburg, and found this in the information on Kiptopeke State Park:<P> Kiptopeke Birding Areas - Since 1963, Kiptopeke has been the site of bird population studies. Sponsored by the Coastal Virginia Wildlife Observatory, formerly known as KESTRSAL, and licensed by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, volunteers capture, examine, weigh, band and release resident and migratory birds in September and October of each year. In the raptor research area, hawks, kestrels, osprey and other birds of prey are observed and banded from September through November. Kiptopeke’s hawk observatory is among the top 15 nationwide.<BR>||||reg||6
